name: slack-api
description: Interact with the Slack Web API to send and update messages, post rich blocks, list channels and conversations, read message history and replies, and look up users. Use whenever Gemini CLI must perform Slack operations such as posting notifications, querying channels or messages, or automating Slack workflows with an existing Bot User OAuth token.
---

# Slack API Interaction

## Prerequisites

- A Slack app/bot user OAuth token (`xoxb-...`) with the scopes needed for the requested operation.
- Read the token from the environment (`SLACK_TOKEN` or `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN`) or an explicit `--token` argument. Never hardcode, generate, or commit tokens; never echo full tokens into outputs.

## Request pattern

Every Web API call is an HTTPS POST to `https://slack.com/api/<method>` with header `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and a `Content-Type: application/json` JSON body. Every response is JSON:

- Success: `{"ok": true, ...result}`
- Failure: `{"ok": false, "error": "<error_code>"}` (HTTP status is usually 200, except `429` on rate limiting)

## Helper script

This skill's canonical request helper is the single-file Node script below. Materialize it at `scripts/slack_request.cjs` inside this skill folder before first use (or whenever this SKILL.md is used standalone), then use it for deterministic calls instead of rewriting request code.

```cjs
#!/usr/bin/env node
'use strict';
// scripts/slack_request.cjs — minimal Slack Web API helper.
// Usage:
//   node slack_request.cjs <api.method> '<json-body>' [--token xoxb-...]
//   SLACK_TOKEN=xoxb-... node slack_request.cjs conversations.list '{"types":"public_channel","limit":100}'
const https = require('https');

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const method = args.shift();
if (!method) {
  console.error('Usage: node slack_request.cjs <api.method> <json-body> [--token xoxb-...]');
  process.exit(2);
}
let token = process.env.SLACK_TOKEN || process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN;
let body = {};
for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === '--token') token = args[++i];
  else {
    try { body = JSON.parse(args[i]); }
    catch (e) { console.error('Invalid JSON body: ' + args[i]); process.exit(2); }
  }
}
if (!token) {
  console.error('Missing Slack token (set --token, SLACK_TOKEN, or SLACK_BOT_TOKEN)');
  process.exit(2);
}
const payload = JSON.stringify(body);
const req = https.request({
  hostname: 'slack.com',
  path: '/api/' + method,
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8',
    'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token,
    'Content-Length': Buffer.byteLength(payload)
  }
}, (res) => {
  let data = '';
  res.on('data', (c) => { data += c; });
  res.on('end', () => {
    if (res.statusCode === 429) {
      console.error('Ratelimited: retry after ' + (res.headers['retry-after'] || '?') + 's');
      process.exit(1);
    }
    if (res.statusCode >= 300) { console.error('HTTP ' + res.statusCode + ': ' + data); process.exit(1); }
    try {
      const out = JSON.parse(data);
      if (!out.ok) { console.error('Slack error: ' + out.error); process.exit(1); }
      console.log(JSON.stringify(out, null, 2));
    } catch (e) { console.error('Non-JSON response: ' + data); process.exit(1); }
  });
});
req.on('error', (e) => { console.error('Request failed: ' + e.message); process.exit(1); });
req.write(payload);
req.end();
```

The helper exits non-zero and prints a one-line error on failure (LLM-friendly stdout); on success it prints the full JSON result. If the script is not yet materialized, fall back to the curl pattern:

```bash
curl -sS https://slack.com/api/chat.postMessage \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SLACK_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"channel":"C0123456789","text":"Hello"}'
```

## Common operations

Required params and the OAuth scopes each call needs. Confirm `ok: true` before using result data.

| Operation | Method | Key parameters | Scope(s) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Post a message | `chat.postMessage` | `channel`, `text` or `blocks`; optional `thread_ts`, `username`, `icon_emoji` | `chat:write` |
| Update a message | `chat.update` | `channel`, `ts`, `text` or `blocks` | `chat:write` |
| Delete a message | `chat.delete` | `channel`, `ts` | `chat:write` |
| List conversations | `conversations.list` | `types` (`public_channel,private_channel,im,mpim`), `limit` (1–200), `cursor` | `channels:read`, `groups:read`, `im:read`, `mpim:read` |
| Read message history | `conversations.history` | `channel`, `limit` (max 200 per page), `cursor`, `oldest`, `latest` (Unix seconds) | same as list |
| Read thread replies | `conversations.replies` | `channel`, `ts` | same as list |
| User by email | `users.lookupByEmail` | `email` | `users:read.email` |
| User info | `users.info` | `user` | `users:read` |
| Add reaction | `reactions.add` | `channel`, `name` (no colons), `timestamp` | `reactions:write` |

## Channel and user addressing

- Prefer channel/user IDs (`C0123...`, `U0123...`, `W0123...`) over names; they are stable and unambiguous.
- Resolve a `#name` or DM target by listing conversations (`conversations.list`) and matching `name`/`is_im`/`user` fields, or resolve a user by email.
- A message is uniquely identified by `channel` + `ts` (the message timestamp string). Use `thread_ts` equal to the parent `ts` to post or read replies in a thread.

## Text formatting (mrkdwn)

- Bold `*text*`, italic `_text_`, code `` `text` ``, strikethrough `~text~`.
- Links: `<https://example.com|Click here>` (pipe separates URL from label).
- Emoji shortcodes like `:white_check_mark:` render natively.
- Encode message text as JSON; never send raw newlines outside of JSON escaping.

## Blocks (rich messages)

- Common block types: `header`, `section`, `divider`, `actions`, `context`.
- Section text uses mrkdwn by default; max 50 blocks per message.
- Length limits: `plain_text` 3000 chars, mrkdwn section text 4000 chars, `header` text 150 chars.
- To attach buttons/menus, add an `actions` block with `elements` of `type: "button"` (etc.) and an `action_id`.

## Pagination

`conversations.list` and `conversations.history` return at most 200 items per page. When a response contains `response_metadata.next_cursor` (non-empty), repeat the call with that value as `cursor` and merge results until `next_cursor` is empty or the requested item count is reached. With the helper: `node slack_request.cjs conversations.list '{"types":"public_channel","limit":200,"cursor":"<next_cursor>"}'`.

## Rate limits and retries

- Slack rate limits calls per method tier (chat.* is tier 3, list/history tier 4, etc.). Tiers are informational; always obey `HTTP 429` responses.
- On `429`, read the `Retry-After` response header (seconds) and wait that long before retrying; additionally back off exponentially (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s) between retries of transient failures.
- Never issue the same request more than ~5 times; report a rate-limit failure instead of looping.

## Errors and troubleshooting

Common `error` codes and fixes:

| Error | Meaning / fix |
|---|---|
| `invalid_auth` | Token missing, malformed, or revoked; check env var and that it starts with `xoxb-` |
| `missing_scope` | App lacks a scope; response lists the required scope(s) — request one, do not guess |
| `channel_not_found` | Channel ID is wrong, or the app is not in that conversation |
| `not_in_channel` | Bot must be invited/added to the channel first (`conversations.invite` or ask a member) |
| `is_archived` | Target channel is archived; use a different channel |
| `thread_not_found` / `message_not_found` | `ts` is stale or from another channel |
| `ratelimited` | HTTP 429 path; honor `Retry-After` |
| `invalid_blocks` | Blocks schema invalid; re-check block JSON against Slack's block kit schema |
| `too_many_attachments` | Too many blocks/attachments; trim under the 50-block limit |

## Validation checklist

Before reporting success, verify all of the following:

1. Response JSON has `"ok": true` (helper exits 0 and prints the result).
2. The intended recipient (channel/user/thread) and `ts` are captured from the response and match the request.
3. For reads, pagination was followed to completion when results exceed one page.
4. No token, secret, or user PII was printed into logs or outputs.

Report results concisely with the actual API response data (e.g., posted message `ts`, matched channels/users), not just that a request was sent.

